# Line endings are part of the invocation contract here, not a formatting preference.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows, where `core.autocrlf=true` is the common default. Without
# the rules below, a fresh clone gets CRLF in every text file -- and two of them stop working:
#
#   bin/voice-tunnel      a bash script. `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` makes the kernel look for an
#                         interpreter literally named "bash\r", and the shim dies with "bad
#                         interpreter: no such file or directory" on a machine where nothing
#                         is actually missing.
#   bin/voice-tunnel.cmd  cmd.exe parses a batch file by byte offset and wants CRLF. It is also
#                         why that file must stay pure ASCII -- see the comment at its top.
#
# Pinning both directions means the shims work the same on a clone as they do here, which is the
# only thing that makes `voice-tunnel <command>` a reliable instruction to give an agent.

* text=auto

bin/voice-tunnel  text eol=lf
*.sh              text eol=lf

*.cmd             text eol=crlf
*.bat             text eol=crlf
